---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const ds = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  '@id': 'https://sinoaihub.com/data-policy/',
  name: 'China AI Hub Data Policy',
  url: 'https://sinoaihub.com/data-policy/',
  description:
    'How China AI Hub licenses and updates its data: reuse terms, main-site vs data-hub division of labor, update cadence, and source principles.',
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Data Policy"
  description="Data licensing, reuse terms, the main site vs Data Hub division of labor, update frequency and source principles for China AI Hub."
  type="article"
  extraJsonLd={[ds]}
  canonicalPath="/data-policy/"
>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>Data Policy</h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">Last updated: 2026-09-29</p>

    <h2>What data this site publishes</h2>
    <p>
      China AI Hub publishes structured reference data on China's AI ecosystem: model
      specifications, pricing, benchmark results, company and agent relationships, and API
      capabilities. Every record is sourced, date-stamped, and carries a verification status. The
      same facts are served two ways, which are covered below.
    </p>

    <h2>Main site vs Data Hub</h2>
    <p>
      The project splits content across two properties on purpose, to keep analysis and data
      separable:
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Division of labor</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Role</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Main site</strong><br /><code>sinoaihub.com</code></td>
          <td>Editorial authority</td>
          <td>Why / what it means / analysis — entity pages, comparisons, guides, research</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Data Hub</strong><br /><code>data.sinoaihub.com</code></td>
          <td>Reference layer</td>
          <td>What is known / exact data / evidence — machine-readable entity records</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Every Data Hub record links back to its canonical main-site page, and the two layers are kept
      in agreement by automated cross-checks. The Data Hub is not a second blog: it carries facts
      and evidence, while interpretation lives on the main site.
    </p>

    <h2>Licensing and reuse</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Original text, analysis and the data structure are the project's intellectual property.</li>
      <li>Short excerpts may be quoted with attribution and a link.</li>
      <li>Bulk scraping or wholesale republishing of the database is not permitted without permission.</li>
      <li>Model names, benchmark names and trademarks belong to their owners and are used for identification only.</li>
      <li>The Data Hub publishes machine-readable reference records for research use; see its pages for applicable terms.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These terms mirror the <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a>, which govern the whole site.
    </p>

    <h2>Update frequency</h2>
    <p>
      Data changes on three cadences, and staleness is shown to the reader rather than hidden:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Weekly (light)</strong> — re-verify pricing and status for the top models and providers.</li>
      <li><strong>Monthly (full)</strong> — crawl all monitored targets and apply validated change proposals.</li>
      <li><strong>Event-driven</strong> — major releases, price cuts or license changes trigger a proposal within 24 hours; the <a href="/news/">news</a> section logs these triggers.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A <code>last_verified</code> date is shown on every record. Under 30 days counts as current;
      30–90 days is flagged "may be outdated"; over 90 days is marked prominently for verification
      before reliance. See the <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> for how verification works.
    </p>

    <h2>Source principles</h2>
    <p>
      Data is gathered from official sources first, then high-quality technical sources, then major
      media, then community — in that order, and community sources never carry a critical fact
      alone. The full hierarchy and the eight-field evidence record are documented in the
      <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> page.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Data errors are corrected through a public process with a written record — see the
      <a href="/corrections/">corrections policy</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
